course: geography
lesson: 2
title: Directions, latitude and longitude
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Every place on Earth has an address made of two numbers: latitude, how far north or south of the equator, and longitude, how far east or west of Greenwich.
explain: Latitude has a natural zero, the equator, halfway between the poles; longitude has none, so in 1884 the world agreed to start it at Greenwich in London.
explain: Latitude was easy for sailors: the higher the Pole Star stands in the sky, the further north you are, a trick Portuguese captains used as they crept down Africa's coast.
explain: Longitude was so hard that ships were wrecked for centuries, until John Harrison's sea clock let a captain compare London time with local noon.
explain: In EU5 your fleets just jump from sea zone to sea zone, but a real captain steered by compass points, kept a written log and could still be badly wrong about where he was.
fact: The four cardinal points are north, east, south and west, and adding north-east, south-east, south-west and north-west makes eight.
fact: A compass points to magnetic north, which is not the same place as the North Pole, and it has been drifting from the Canadian Arctic towards Siberia by tens of kilometres a year.
fact: Latitude measures how far north or south of the equator a place is, from 0 degrees at the equator to 90 degrees at each pole.
fact: One degree of latitude is about 111 km anywhere on Earth.
fact: Longitude measures how far east or west a place is from the prime meridian, which runs through Greenwich in London and was agreed at a conference in Washington in 1884.
fact: Lines of longitude, called meridians, run from pole to pole and all meet at the poles, so one degree of longitude is about 111 km wide at the equator but shrinks to nothing at the poles; at London it is only about 69 km.
fact: In the northern hemisphere, the height of the Pole Star above the horizon in degrees is roughly equal to your latitude.
fact: For centuries sailors could not find their longitude at sea, until John Harrison built an accurate marine clock, his H4 watch, which was tested at sea in 1761; the Earth turns 15 degrees every hour, so each hour between London time and local noon means 15 degrees of longitude.
fact: The Tropic of Cancer lies at about 23.4 degrees north, the Tropic of Capricorn at about 23.4 degrees south and the Arctic Circle at about 66.6 degrees north; these lines come from the Earth's tilt of about 23.4 degrees (90 minus 23.4 is 66.6).
fact: London lies at about 51.5 degrees north and 0.1 degrees west, so a pair of numbers can locate any place on Earth.
fact: The Sun rises in the east and sets in the west everywhere on Earth, because the Earth spins from west to east.
fact: In EU5 the far north has cold, harsh locations and the tropics hot ones, which follows latitude quite well (the further from the equator, the lower and weaker the sunlight), though real climate also depends on seas, winds and height.
exercise: What direction lies exactly halfway between south and west? | South-west | North-west | South-east | West-north
exercise: How many points does a compass show if you add the in-between directions to the four cardinal ones? | Eight | Only six | Twelve | Sixteen
exercise: Where does a compass needle point? | To magnetic north, not the North Pole itself | Exactly to the North Pole, wherever you stand | To the Pole Star | To the nearest big mountain made of iron rock
exercise: Which way has magnetic north been drifting? | From the Canadian Arctic towards Siberia | From Siberia towards the Canadian Arctic | From Greenland towards Iceland | It has never moved
exercise: What is the latitude of the North Pole? | 90 degrees north | 0 degrees | 180 degrees | 360 degrees
exercise: What does latitude measure? | How far north or south of the equator a place is | How far east or west of Greenwich a place is | How high a place is above the level of the sea | How far a place is from the sea
exercise: About how far apart are two places 1 degree of latitude apart? | About 111 km | About 11 km | About 1,111 km | About 1 km
exercise: Through which place does the prime meridian run? | Greenwich in London | Paris | Washington | The North Pole only
exercise: In which year was the prime meridian agreed? | 1884 | 1492 | 1761 | 1945
exercise: How wide is one degree of longitude at the poles? | Nothing at all | About 111 km | About 222 km | About 69 km
exercise: How can a sailor in the north find his latitude at night? | Measure how high the Pole Star stands | Count the stars he can see in the sky | Watch exactly where the Moon sets each night | Measure the depth of the sea
exercise: Who solved the problem of longitude at sea with a clock? | John Harrison | James Cook | Gerardus Mercator | Isaac Newton
exercise: When was Harrison's H4 watch tested at sea? | In 1761 | In 1337 | In 1884 | In 1066
exercise: At about what latitude is the Arctic Circle? | About 66.6 degrees north | About 23.4 degrees north | About 51.5 degrees north | About 90 degrees north
exercise: At about what latitude is the Tropic of Capricorn? | About 23.4 degrees south | About 23.4 degrees north | About 66.6 degrees south | About 45 degrees south
exercise: Which number in London's address, 51.5 N and 0.1 W, tells how far north it is? | 51.5, the latitude | 0.1, the longitude west | Neither, you need a third number | Both added together
exercise: Where does the Sun rise in Australia? | In the east, as everywhere | In the west | In the north | In the south
test: Why is there a natural zero for latitude but not for longitude? | The equator lies halfway between the poles, but every meridian is alike | The equator is hotter than other places | Greenwich is the oldest city with an observatory | Longitude lines are curved and latitude lines are straight, so they cannot start at zero
test: Before 1884 the French counted longitude from Paris. What problem did that cause? | Maps from different countries gave different numbers for one place | Ships could not sail to France | Latitude stopped working on French maps and charts | The Sun seemed to rise in the west for anyone sailing to France
test: Two towns are 2 degrees of latitude apart on the same meridian. Roughly how far apart are they? | About 222 km | About 22 km | About 2,220 km | About 111 km
test: Your ship's clock, set to London time, reads 3 p.m. when the Sun is highest where you are. How far west of London are you? | 45 degrees | 3 degrees | 15 degrees | 90 degrees
test: Where on Earth would the Pole Star sit right on the horizon? | At the equator | At the North Pole | In London, all year round | At the Arctic Circle
test: Bro says the Arctic Circle is at 66.6 cos it's just a random number. True? | No, it is 90 minus the tilt of 23.4 degrees | Yes, it was picked by a king of Norway long ago | Yes, it changes every year by a lot | No, it is exactly twice the tropic line
test: Why would an old map's compass instructions slowly become wrong? | Magnetic north keeps moving | The paper shrinks over time | The Earth's poles swap every year | Compasses wear out with use
test: If the Earth spun the other way, where would the Sun rise? | In the west | Still in the east | In the north | It would not rise at all
test: You walk around the world near the North Pole. Why do you cross every longitude in a few steps? | All the meridians meet at the pole | Longitude does not exist in the Arctic | The ice moves you along | Degrees are bigger near the poles
test: In EU5 the northern locations are cold and poor. What real thing does the game copy there? | Higher latitudes get weaker sunlight | Northern lands are higher above the sea | The north is further from the Sun in summer | The game is just guessing about the weather
test: How could Portuguese captains know how far south they had sailed along Africa? | From the height of the Pole Star, sinking lower each night | From the colour of the sea water | By counting the days only | By the length of the coast already drawn on their charts at home
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the determination of longitude at sea remained impracticable prior to the eighteenth century. | No sufficiently accurate marine timekeeper existed | The magnetic compass had not been invented | The Pole Star was not visible from ships | Latitude had not yet been defined
exam: Ascertain the approximate distance corresponding to a single degree of latitude upon the Earth's surface. | One hundred and eleven kilometres | Eleven kilometres | Sixty-nine kilometres | One thousand kilometres
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the prime meridian? | It passes through Greenwich and was so agreed in 1884 | It passes through Paris and was so agreed in 1884 | It coincides with the equator | It passes through Washington
exam: It is given that an observer in the northern hemisphere measures the Pole Star at an elevation of fifty degrees. Determine the observer's approximate latitude. | Fifty degrees north | Forty degrees north | Fifty degrees south | Ninety degrees north
